1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,690
Sweetheart, for twenty-six winters,

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:05,457
I knitted mittens for every child at

3
00:00:05,457 --> 00:00:08,686
Lincoln Elementary who came to school with

4
00:00:08,686 --> 00:00:09,531
bare hands.

5
00:00:09,711 --> 00:00:11,813
Nobody asked me to.

6
00:00:11,993 --> 00:00:14,322
Most people thought I was a silly

7
00:00:14,322 --> 00:00:16,370
old woman with too much yarn.

8
00:00:16,550 --> 00:00:18,806
And then, on Christmas Eve of two

9
00:00:18,806 --> 00:00:21,815
thousand twenty-three, a black car pulled up

10
00:00:21,815 --> 00:00:23,251
in front of my house,

11
00:00:23,251 --> 00:00:25,097
and a man got out holding a

12
00:00:25,097 --> 00:00:27,353
pair of red mittens I had knitted

13
00:00:27,353 --> 00:00:28,926
in nineteen eighty-one.

14
00:00:29,276 --> 00:00:31,541
Tonight I'm going to tell you how

15
00:00:31,541 --> 00:00:33,944
it started, the little boy who wore

16
00:00:33,944 --> 00:00:35,180
those red mittens,

17
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:36,896
and what he told me on my

18
00:00:36,896 --> 00:00:38,819
porch forty-two years later.

19
00:00:38,999 --> 00:00:40,098
Stay with me,

20
00:00:40,098 --> 00:00:43,142
honey, because what he did next made

21
00:00:43,142 --> 00:00:45,593
the evening news in Columbus.

22
00:00:45,773 --> 00:00:47,718
And I still can't talk about it

23
00:00:47,718 --> 00:00:49,663
without needing a handkerchief.

24
00:00:50,013 --> 00:00:52,497
It started in nineteen seventy-eight.

25
00:00:52,677 --> 00:00:55,414
I was walking past Lincoln Elementary on

26
00:00:55,414 --> 00:00:57,467
a morning so cold the air hurt

27
00:00:57,467 --> 00:00:59,519
your teeth, and I saw a little

28
00:00:59,519 --> 00:01:01,504
girl at the bus stop with her

29
00:01:01,504 --> 00:01:04,241
hands pulled up inside her coat sleeves.

30
00:01:04,421 --> 00:01:05,733
No mittens.

31
00:01:05,913 --> 00:01:08,409
Her fingers were red and cracked.

32
00:01:08,589 --> 00:01:10,038
And I asked her,

33
00:01:10,038 --> 00:01:12,757
honey, where are your mittens?

34
00:01:12,937 --> 00:01:13,923
And she said,

35
00:01:13,923 --> 00:01:15,897
my brother has them today.

36
00:01:16,077 --> 00:01:17,424
We take turns.

37
00:01:17,774 --> 00:01:19,318
We take turns.

38
00:01:19,498 --> 00:01:21,900
I went straight home and dug out

39
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:24,002
every scrap of yarn I owned.

40
00:01:24,182 --> 00:01:26,628
Grandma Pearl had taught me to knit

41
00:01:26,628 --> 00:01:27,816
when I was seven.

42
00:01:27,996 --> 00:01:30,597
That night, I made her a pair

43
00:01:30,597 --> 00:01:31,584
of mittens.

44
00:01:31,764 --> 00:01:34,417
Purple, because it was all I had

45
00:01:34,417 --> 00:01:35,247
enough of.

46
00:01:35,427 --> 00:01:36,903
And the next morning,

47
00:01:36,903 --> 00:01:38,660
I gave them to her at the

48
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:39,293
bus stop.

49
00:01:39,473 --> 00:01:41,278
She put them on and held her

50
00:01:41,278 --> 00:01:43,212
hands up to her face like they

51
00:01:43,212 --> 00:01:44,372
were made of gold.

52
00:01:44,722 --> 00:01:46,964
Then I went to the principal,

53
00:01:46,964 --> 00:01:47,195
Mr.

54
00:01:47,375 --> 00:01:49,732
Daniels, and I asked him how many

55
00:01:49,732 --> 00:01:52,588
children at Lincoln didn't have mittens.

56
00:01:52,768 --> 00:01:55,130
He looked at me and said,

57
00:01:55,130 --> 00:01:55,508
Mrs.

58
00:01:55,688 --> 00:01:58,126
Miller, about sixty.

59
00:01:58,306 --> 00:01:59,794
And I thought,

60
00:01:59,794 --> 00:02:00,431
sixty.

61
00:02:00,611 --> 00:02:02,097
I can do sixty.

62
00:02:02,277 --> 00:02:04,506
It takes me about three hours to

63
00:02:04,506 --> 00:02:05,342
knit a pair.

64
00:02:05,522 --> 00:02:07,676
In a minute I'll tell you how

65
00:02:07,676 --> 00:02:09,829
many pairs I ended up making.

66
00:02:10,009 --> 00:02:12,738
I didn't believe the number myself.

67
00:02:13,087 --> 00:02:15,379
I knitted every night that winter,

68
00:02:15,379 --> 00:02:18,277
sweetheart, after the children were in bed.

69
00:02:18,457 --> 00:02:20,693
Harold would read the paper,

70
00:02:20,693 --> 00:02:21,731
and I'd knit.

71
00:02:21,911 --> 00:02:23,624
Red, blue, green,

72
00:02:23,624 --> 00:02:27,252
striped, whatever yarn I could find.

73
00:02:27,432 --> 00:02:29,998
I bought yarn at rummage sales.

74
00:02:30,178 --> 00:02:32,569
I unraveled old sweaters.

75
00:02:32,749 --> 00:02:35,926
Ladies at church started bringing me bags

76
00:02:35,926 --> 00:02:36,546
of yarn.

77
00:02:36,726 --> 00:02:38,179
And every Monday,

78
00:02:38,179 --> 00:02:40,915
I took a grocery sack of mittens

79
00:02:40,915 --> 00:02:41,428
to Mr.

80
00:02:41,608 --> 00:02:43,523
Daniels's office.

81
00:02:43,873 --> 00:02:46,986
The children started calling me the mitten

82
00:02:46,986 --> 00:02:47,356
lady.

83
00:02:47,536 --> 00:02:49,935
When I walked past the playground,

84
00:02:49,935 --> 00:02:52,192
they'd wave their mittens at me.

85
00:02:52,372 --> 00:02:54,760
Some of the other mothers at church

86
00:02:54,760 --> 00:02:55,852
weren't so kind,

87
00:02:55,852 --> 00:02:56,261
honey.

88
00:02:56,441 --> 00:02:57,518
One said, Ruth,

89
00:02:57,518 --> 00:03:00,534
those people should buy their own children

90
00:03:00,534 --> 00:03:01,109
mittens.

91
00:03:01,289 --> 00:03:03,193
You're just encouraging them.

92
00:03:03,373 --> 00:03:04,791
And another said,

93
00:03:04,791 --> 00:03:06,960
it's a waste of good yarn.

94
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:08,986
They'll lose them in a week.

95
00:03:09,336 --> 00:03:12,415
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

96
00:03:12,415 --> 00:03:14,789
tap the like button for me,

97
00:03:14,789 --> 00:03:15,757
sweetheart.

98
00:03:15,937 --> 00:03:19,095
It helps more people find these stories.

99
00:03:19,275 --> 00:03:22,177
And yes, some of them lost them.

100
00:03:22,357 --> 00:03:24,064
Children lose mittens.

101
00:03:24,244 --> 00:03:26,125
That's what children do.

102
00:03:26,305 --> 00:03:27,907
So I knitted more.

103
00:03:28,087 --> 00:03:31,152
I sewed little name tags inside.

104
00:03:31,332 --> 00:03:33,398
And I tied them together with a

105
00:03:33,398 --> 00:03:35,865
long string through the coat sleeves,

106
00:03:35,865 --> 00:03:37,798
the way my mother did for me,

107
00:03:37,798 --> 00:03:39,598
so they couldn't lose them.

108
00:03:39,948 --> 00:03:42,633
And here's how I knew it mattered,

109
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:43,106
honey.

110
00:03:43,286 --> 00:03:45,492
In February, Mr.

111
00:03:45,672 --> 00:03:48,911
Daniels called me into his office.

112
00:03:49,091 --> 00:03:51,146
He said, Mrs.

113
00:03:51,326 --> 00:03:54,128
Miller, our absences are down by a

114
00:03:54,128 --> 00:03:55,611
third this winter.

115
00:03:55,791 --> 00:03:58,435
The nurse says she's seeing half the

116
00:03:58,435 --> 00:03:59,169
frostbite.

117
00:03:59,349 --> 00:04:01,577
And the children are coming to school

118
00:04:01,577 --> 00:04:02,782
on the coldest days,

119
00:04:02,782 --> 00:04:05,010
because they're not afraid of the bus

120
00:04:05,010 --> 00:04:05,793
stop anymore.

121
00:04:05,973 --> 00:04:08,028
Mittens, sweetheart.

122
00:04:08,208 --> 00:04:09,601
Just mittens.

123
00:04:09,950 --> 00:04:12,603
In the winter of nineteen eighty-one,

124
00:04:12,603 --> 00:04:14,681
there was a boy in the second

125
00:04:14,681 --> 00:04:16,545
grade named Tommy Kessler.

126
00:04:16,725 --> 00:04:18,912
Tommy was small for his age,

127
00:04:18,912 --> 00:04:21,333
and quiet, and he wore the same

128
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:23,598
thin jacket every single day.

129
00:04:23,778 --> 00:04:25,357
His father had left.

130
00:04:25,537 --> 00:04:28,329
His mother cleaned motel rooms out on

131
00:04:28,329 --> 00:04:29,310
Route Thirty.

132
00:04:29,490 --> 00:04:31,837
And Tommy had a habit that broke

133
00:04:31,837 --> 00:04:32,497
my heart.

134
00:04:32,677 --> 00:04:34,769
He'd stand at the back of the

135
00:04:34,769 --> 00:04:36,067
line every Monday,

136
00:04:36,067 --> 00:04:37,437
and he'd never ask.

137
00:04:37,787 --> 00:04:40,739
Every other child would crowd around the

138
00:04:40,739 --> 00:04:41,108
sack.

139
00:04:41,288 --> 00:04:43,051
Tommy would stand back,

140
00:04:43,051 --> 00:04:45,351
with his hands in his pockets,

141
00:04:45,351 --> 00:04:46,118
and watch.

142
00:04:46,298 --> 00:04:47,439
So one Monday,

143
00:04:47,439 --> 00:04:49,722
I knelt down in front of him

144
00:04:49,722 --> 00:04:51,027
and said, Tommy,

145
00:04:51,027 --> 00:04:53,229
what's your favorite color?

146
00:04:53,409 --> 00:04:55,185
And he whispered,

147
00:04:55,185 --> 00:04:55,603
red.

148
00:04:55,783 --> 00:04:57,281
Like a fire truck.

149
00:04:57,461 --> 00:04:58,399
So that week,

150
00:04:58,399 --> 00:05:00,780
I knitted Tommy Kessler a pair of

151
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:03,450
red mittens, with a little white star

152
00:05:03,450 --> 00:05:05,181
on the back of each one.

153
00:05:05,531 --> 00:05:07,375
When I gave them to him,

154
00:05:07,375 --> 00:05:09,525
honey, he didn't say a word.

155
00:05:09,705 --> 00:05:10,700
He put them on,

156
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:12,956
and he looked at the little stars,

157
00:05:12,956 --> 00:05:15,145
and then he threw his arms around

158
00:05:15,145 --> 00:05:17,069
my neck so hard he knocked my

159
00:05:17,069 --> 00:05:17,600
hat off.

160
00:05:17,780 --> 00:05:20,337
And then he ran off across the

161
00:05:20,337 --> 00:05:23,320
playground with his hands up in the

162
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:25,536
air, yelling, I got stars!

163
00:05:25,716 --> 00:05:27,353
I got stars!

164
00:05:27,533 --> 00:05:29,437
I never forgot that.

165
00:05:29,617 --> 00:05:32,277
And I never saw Tommy Kessler again

166
00:05:32,277 --> 00:05:33,645
after that spring.

167
00:05:33,995 --> 00:05:36,894
His mother moved them away that summer,

168
00:05:36,894 --> 00:05:39,570
sweetheart, to somewhere in Indiana,

169
00:05:39,570 --> 00:05:41,205
and nobody knew where.

170
00:05:41,385 --> 00:05:42,837
I kept knitting.

171
00:05:43,017 --> 00:05:44,665
Twenty-six winters.

172
00:05:44,845 --> 00:05:47,097
When my hands got too stiff with

173
00:05:47,097 --> 00:05:49,983
arthritis, the ladies at church took over

174
00:05:49,983 --> 00:05:50,969
half the work,

175
00:05:50,969 --> 00:05:52,728
and I did the other half.

176
00:05:52,908 --> 00:05:54,631
When I finally stopped,

177
00:05:54,631 --> 00:05:56,205
in two thousand four,

178
00:05:56,205 --> 00:05:59,126
the principal at Lincoln counted up the

179
00:05:59,126 --> 00:06:00,025
old records.

180
00:06:00,375 --> 00:06:03,577
Four thousand one hundred and twelve pairs

181
00:06:03,577 --> 00:06:04,950
of mittens, honey.

182
00:06:05,130 --> 00:06:06,117
Four thousand.

183
00:06:06,297 --> 00:06:08,479
I sat down when he told me.

184
00:06:08,659 --> 00:06:10,784
I thought maybe a thousand.

185
00:06:10,964 --> 00:06:13,777
Four thousand pairs of little hands that

186
00:06:13,777 --> 00:06:15,887
didn't freeze at the bus stop.

187
00:06:16,067 --> 00:06:18,072
And the lady from church who'd said

188
00:06:18,072 --> 00:06:19,619
they'd lose them in a week?

189
00:06:19,799 --> 00:06:21,697
She came up to me at the

190
00:06:21,697 --> 00:06:23,357
coffee hour and said,

191
00:06:23,357 --> 00:06:24,780
Ruth, I was wrong.

192
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:27,385
And she handed me a bag of

193
00:06:27,385 --> 00:06:27,851
yarn.

194
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:30,032
And every single winter,

195
00:06:30,032 --> 00:06:32,322
I thought about Tommy Kessler.

196
00:06:32,502 --> 00:06:34,632
I prayed for him at Christmas,

197
00:06:34,632 --> 00:06:37,329
honey, along with all the others whose

198
00:06:37,329 --> 00:06:39,317
names I'd sewn into mittens.

199
00:06:39,497 --> 00:06:41,494
I wondered if he was warm.

200
00:06:41,674 --> 00:06:44,648
If anybody ever asked him his favorite

201
00:06:44,648 --> 00:06:45,587
color again.

202
00:06:45,767 --> 00:06:48,324
If those red mittens had made it

203
00:06:48,324 --> 00:06:49,203
to Indiana.

204
00:06:49,383 --> 00:06:50,880
Harold used to say,

205
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:53,400
Ruthie, you can't save them all.

206
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:54,561
And I'd say,

207
00:06:54,561 --> 00:06:57,014
no, but I can keep their hands

208
00:06:57,014 --> 00:06:57,423
warm.

209
00:06:57,773 --> 00:06:59,573
So far, the bus stop,

210
00:06:59,573 --> 00:07:02,228
the sacks, and the boy with the

211
00:07:02,228 --> 00:07:02,743
stars.

212
00:07:02,923 --> 00:07:06,240
On Christmas Eve of two thousand twenty-three,

213
00:07:06,240 --> 00:07:07,610
I was seventy-nine,

214
00:07:07,610 --> 00:07:10,494
and alone, because Harold had passed and

215
00:07:10,494 --> 00:07:13,163
my children were coming the next day.

216
00:07:13,343 --> 00:07:14,661
At four o'clock,

217
00:07:14,661 --> 00:07:17,049
a long black car pulled up in

218
00:07:17,049 --> 00:07:18,532
front of my house.

219
00:07:18,712 --> 00:07:20,974
A man in a dark wool coat

220
00:07:20,974 --> 00:07:22,875
got out, maybe fifty,

221
00:07:22,875 --> 00:07:24,865
with gray in his hair.

222
00:07:25,045 --> 00:07:27,367
And he was carrying a little frame.

223
00:07:27,717 --> 00:07:29,742
When I opened the door,

224
00:07:29,742 --> 00:07:30,887
he said, Mrs.

225
00:07:31,067 --> 00:07:31,833
Miller?

226
00:07:32,013 --> 00:07:33,348
The mitten lady?

227
00:07:33,528 --> 00:07:35,514
And he held up the frame.

228
00:07:35,694 --> 00:07:37,538
Inside, under glass,

229
00:07:37,538 --> 00:07:40,396
was a pair of tiny red mittens.

230
00:07:40,576 --> 00:07:41,806
Worn thin.

231
00:07:41,986 --> 00:07:44,033
With a little white star on the

232
00:07:44,033 --> 00:07:45,156
back of each one.

233
00:07:45,336 --> 00:07:47,341
I knew them instantly,

234
00:07:47,341 --> 00:07:48,343
sweetheart.

235
00:07:48,523 --> 00:07:50,938
I'd know my own knitting anywhere.

236
00:07:51,118 --> 00:07:53,755
And I looked up at his face,

237
00:07:53,755 --> 00:07:54,790
and I said,

238
00:07:54,790 --> 00:07:55,355
Tommy?

239
00:07:55,535 --> 00:07:56,685
Tommy Kessler?

240
00:07:57,034 --> 00:07:58,968
He came in and sat at my

241
00:07:58,968 --> 00:08:02,271
kitchen table, and he told me everything.

242
00:08:02,451 --> 00:08:04,921
After they moved to Indiana,

243
00:08:04,921 --> 00:08:06,421
things got worse.

244
00:08:06,601 --> 00:08:08,169
His mother got sick.

245
00:08:08,349 --> 00:08:11,309
He spent two years in foster homes.

246
00:08:11,489 --> 00:08:12,882
And he said,

247
00:08:12,882 --> 00:08:13,347
Mrs.

248
00:08:13,527 --> 00:08:16,044
Miller, I didn't have much in those

249
00:08:16,044 --> 00:08:16,476
years.

250
00:08:16,656 --> 00:08:18,903
I didn't have a coat that fit,

251
00:08:18,903 --> 00:08:20,626
or a bed that was mine.

252
00:08:20,806 --> 00:08:23,093
But I had those red mittens.

253
00:08:23,273 --> 00:08:25,235
I carried them everywhere,

254
00:08:25,235 --> 00:08:26,292
in every move.

255
00:08:26,641 --> 00:08:29,580
He said, when things got really bad,

256
00:08:29,580 --> 00:08:32,192
I'd look at the little stars and

257
00:08:32,192 --> 00:08:35,539
think, somebody once knelt down and asked

258
00:08:35,539 --> 00:08:37,253
me my favorite color.

259
00:08:37,433 --> 00:08:39,860
Somebody I wasn't related to,

260
00:08:39,860 --> 00:08:41,450
who didn't have to.

261
00:08:41,630 --> 00:08:44,282
That meant somebody thought I was worth

262
00:08:44,282 --> 00:08:44,486
it.

263
00:08:44,666 --> 00:08:46,689
I held on to that,

264
00:08:46,689 --> 00:08:47,139
Mrs.

265
00:08:47,319 --> 00:08:48,283
Miller.

266
00:08:48,463 --> 00:08:51,478
Some nights, that was the only thing

267
00:08:51,478 --> 00:08:53,153
I had to hold on to.

268
00:08:53,503 --> 00:08:54,836
Tommy grew up,

269
00:08:54,836 --> 00:08:55,407
honey.

270
00:08:55,587 --> 00:08:58,523
He worked his way through Purdue University

271
00:08:58,523 --> 00:08:59,546
washing dishes.

272
00:08:59,726 --> 00:09:01,387
He became an engineer.

273
00:09:01,567 --> 00:09:03,829
And then he started a company that

274
00:09:03,829 --> 00:09:05,294
makes heating systems.

275
00:09:05,474 --> 00:09:07,447
Furnaces and boilers.

276
00:09:07,627 --> 00:09:09,561
He said, I guess I spent my

277
00:09:09,561 --> 00:09:12,354
whole life trying to make sure nobody's

278
00:09:12,354 --> 00:09:12,712
cold.

279
00:09:12,892 --> 00:09:14,101
And his company,

280
00:09:14,101 --> 00:09:15,461
I found out later,

281
00:09:15,461 --> 00:09:18,709
was worth over two hundred million dollars.

282
00:09:19,058 --> 00:09:21,003
And he told me about the day

283
00:09:21,003 --> 00:09:22,669
he finally outgrew them.

284
00:09:22,849 --> 00:09:23,740
He was nine,

285
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:26,042
in a foster home in Fort Wayne,

286
00:09:26,042 --> 00:09:28,715
and the mittens wouldn't go over his

287
00:09:28,715 --> 00:09:29,978
knuckles anymore.

288
00:09:30,158 --> 00:09:32,639
He said, I cried harder that day

289
00:09:32,639 --> 00:09:34,732
than when I left my mother.

290
00:09:34,912 --> 00:09:36,940
So his foster mother,

291
00:09:36,940 --> 00:09:39,161
a kind woman named Mrs.

292
00:09:39,341 --> 00:09:41,973
Ortiz, took them and put them in

293
00:09:41,973 --> 00:09:43,289
a frame for him.

294
00:09:43,469 --> 00:09:45,559
And he'd had them on his office

295
00:09:45,559 --> 00:09:46,638
wall ever since.

296
00:09:46,988 --> 00:09:49,372
Then Tommy took out an envelope and

297
00:09:49,372 --> 00:09:51,551
slid it across my kitchen table.

298
00:09:51,731 --> 00:09:53,670
He said, Mrs.

299
00:09:53,850 --> 00:09:56,745
Miller, I went to Lincoln Elementary this

300
00:09:56,745 --> 00:09:57,310
morning.

301
00:09:57,490 --> 00:09:58,848
It's still there.

302
00:09:59,028 --> 00:10:00,538
It's still poor.

303
00:10:00,718 --> 00:10:03,156
Seventy percent of the kids are on

304
00:10:03,156 --> 00:10:03,945
free lunch.

305
00:10:04,125 --> 00:10:06,099
And I made them a promise.

306
00:10:06,279 --> 00:10:09,106
Inside that envelope was a letter from

307
00:10:09,106 --> 00:10:09,924
the school.

308
00:10:10,104 --> 00:10:12,712
Tommy's company had paid for a brand-new

309
00:10:12,712 --> 00:10:15,189
heating system for the whole building.

310
00:10:15,540 --> 00:10:17,305
And not only that,

311
00:10:17,305 --> 00:10:18,384
sweetheart.

312
00:10:18,564 --> 00:10:21,524
Every child at Lincoln Elementary,

313
00:10:21,524 --> 00:10:24,485
every year, for as long as Tommy's

314
00:10:24,485 --> 00:10:27,881
company exists, gets a new winter coat,

315
00:10:27,881 --> 00:10:29,013
boots, a hat,

316
00:10:29,013 --> 00:10:30,929
and a pair of mittens.

317
00:10:31,109 --> 00:10:32,769
He'd set up a fund.

318
00:10:32,949 --> 00:10:34,238
And he'd named it.

319
00:10:34,418 --> 00:10:37,172
I didn't find out until the newspaper

320
00:10:37,172 --> 00:10:40,002
ran the story the day after Christmas.

321
00:10:40,182 --> 00:10:42,574
He called it the Mitten Lady Fund.

322
00:10:42,754 --> 00:10:44,983
With my picture on the website.

323
00:10:45,333 --> 00:10:47,906
The Columbus news came out to Lincoln

324
00:10:47,906 --> 00:10:50,689
to film the children getting their coats

325
00:10:50,689 --> 00:10:53,054
that January, and they asked me to

326
00:10:53,054 --> 00:10:53,680
be there.

327
00:10:53,860 --> 00:10:56,075
I sat in a folding chair in

328
00:10:56,075 --> 00:10:59,438
the gym, and four hundred children walked

329
00:10:59,438 --> 00:11:01,079
past me, one by one,

330
00:11:01,079 --> 00:11:02,637
in their new coats.

331
00:11:02,817 --> 00:11:04,773
And every single one of them was

332
00:11:04,773 --> 00:11:06,852
wearing a pair of red mittens with

333
00:11:06,852 --> 00:11:08,808
a little white star on the back.

334
00:11:08,988 --> 00:11:11,043
Tommy had them made special.

335
00:11:11,393 --> 00:11:13,982
Tommy calls me every Sunday now.

336
00:11:14,162 --> 00:11:16,227
He and his wife and their three

337
00:11:16,227 --> 00:11:18,492
children come every Christmas Eve.

338
00:11:18,672 --> 00:11:19,893
And every year,

339
00:11:19,893 --> 00:11:22,090
I knit one more pair of red

340
00:11:22,090 --> 00:11:24,125
mittens with white stars,

341
00:11:24,125 --> 00:11:26,729
and I give them to his youngest.

342
00:11:26,909 --> 00:11:27,792
She's nine.

343
00:11:27,972 --> 00:11:29,995
She says when she grows up,

344
00:11:29,995 --> 00:11:32,467
she's going to be the mitten lady

345
00:11:32,467 --> 00:11:32,767
too.

346
00:11:32,947 --> 00:11:36,209
I told her she'd better start practicing.

347
00:11:36,559 --> 00:11:39,274
Here's what I want you to remember,

348
00:11:39,274 --> 00:11:39,740
honey.

349
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:42,769
You never know which small kindness is

350
00:11:42,769 --> 00:11:45,318
going to be the one somebody holds

351
00:11:45,318 --> 00:11:46,967
on to for forty years.

352
00:11:47,147 --> 00:11:48,784
A pair of mittens.

353
00:11:48,964 --> 00:11:50,113
A sandwich.

354
00:11:50,293 --> 00:11:53,486
Asking a quiet child their favorite color.

355
00:11:53,666 --> 00:11:55,570
Do the small thing anyway,

356
00:11:55,570 --> 00:11:57,840
even if people say it's a waste

357
00:11:57,840 --> 00:11:58,426
of yarn.

358
00:11:58,606 --> 00:12:00,290
Especially then.

359
00:12:00,639 --> 00:12:02,351
Tell me in the comments,

360
00:12:02,351 --> 00:12:03,136
sweetheart.

361
00:12:03,316 --> 00:12:06,207
Was there somebody like the mitten lady

362
00:12:06,207 --> 00:12:07,170
in your life?

363
00:12:07,350 --> 00:12:09,448
A teacher, a neighbor,

364
00:12:09,448 --> 00:12:10,496
a stranger?

365
00:12:10,676 --> 00:12:12,569
I'd love to hear about them.

366
00:12:12,749 --> 00:12:15,104
There are more of my family's stories

367
00:12:15,104 --> 00:12:17,332
in the playlist right there on your

368
00:12:17,332 --> 00:12:19,305
screen, and I'll see you in the

369
00:12:19,305 --> 00:12:19,877
next one.

370
00:12:20,057 --> 00:12:21,625
Good night, honey.

371
00:12:21,805 --> 00:12:23,268
Merry Christmas.
